QPE is one of the most important quantum simulation
algorithms [8], which relates Hamiltonian time-evolution
U (t) = e−itH to the measurement of energy eigenvalues [9–11]. In the ideal version of the algorithm, measuring the output of the phase estimation circuit collapses
the system into an energy eigenstate of H. If we replace
the time evolution with the product formula approximation T (δt)L , then (in the ideal case) we will instead measure an energy eigenvalue of e
H. Therefore the Trotter
error is directly related to the difference in spectrum between H and e
H. Under conditions which are satisfied in
many cases of interest, we show that the first order perturbative correction vanishes and use this to rigorously
show that the Trotter error in phase can be reduced from
O(Lδt2 ) to O(Lδt3 ). In terms of the target precision  of
the QPE, this means the Trotter step size can be enlarged
from δt = O() to δt = O(1/2 ).

2
DAS can be used to implement adiabatic quantum
computation [12–14] and adiabatic state preparation [15,
16] on a digital quantum computer. The scaling of Trotter error in DAS has previously been analyzed in terms
of the operator norm [17, 18]. We find this measure of
error is dominated by the accumulation of a global phase,
whereas in adiabatic algorithms it is generally only the
fidelity error f that matters. We regard the Trotterized
time evolution as a discretized adiabatic evolution under
an effective Hamiltonian, and obtain tighter bounds on
the fidelity error by applying an adiabatic theorem to the
effective Hamiltonian.
This paper is organized as follows: we first illustrate
the setting of the problems and our main results, together
with the techniques and lemmas used in the proof. The
primary mathematical tools are a rigorous perturbation
method [19] and the Magnus expansion [20]. Then we apply the main results to analyze Trotter error in QPE and
DAS, in both cases we achieve improvements in circuit
complexity.
